.Dd September 19, 2026
.Dt PASSWD 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm passwd
.Nd change an account's password
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Ss os-simple
.Nm
.Op Fl Fl
.Op Ar user
.Ss os-real
.Nm
.Op Fl a Ar algorithm
.Op Ar user
.Nm
.Op Fl a Ar algorithm
.Fl d | Fl l | Fl u
.Ar user
.Sh DESCRIPTION
On os-simple,
.Nm
is the project program, installed set-user-ID root.
The account defaults to the caller's own account.
Changing another account's password requires a real user ID of 0.
Root need not supply the old password; other callers must enter the
account's current password first.
.Pp
The new password is entered twice.
Passwords are limited to 100 bytes.
After the prompts,
.Nm
locks and rechecks the account records before replacing
.Pa /etc/shadow .
The lock, temporary file, and rename are described in
.Xr passwd 5 .
.Pp
On os-real,
.Nm
is BusyBox's applet from the set-id build.
It accepts the options below.
.Sh OPTIONS
The project program accepts
.Fl Fl
to end option processing before
.Ar user .
.Pp
The BusyBox applet accepts:
.Bl -tag -width "-a algorithm"
.It Fl a Ar algorithm
Select the crypt algorithm.
.It Fl d
Set an empty password.
.It Fl l
Lock the account.
.It Fl u
Unlock the account.
.El
.Pp
The three operations require a real user ID of 0 and name the
.Ar user ;
BusyBox refuses them without the operand.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /etc/shadow
.It Pa /etc/passwd
Account records.
.It Pa /etc/shadow
Password records.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Dl passwd
The
.Ql tests/run account
suite changes a password and logs in with it.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr login 1 ,
.Xr su 1 ,
.Xr passwd 5 ,
.Xr adduser 8
.Sh HISTORY
